Dear Orange County Voter: Thank you for being registered to vote so your voice can be heard regarding how your community is governed and by whom. As your Registrar of Voters, it is my commitment that Orange County elections are fair, accurate, transparent, accessible, secure, and comply with federal and state laws. This Voter Information Guide explains how, when, and where to vote in the November 3, 2026 General Election. Please take a moment to review how to “Vote Easy. Vote Secure.” in this election: • Mail Ballots: Pursuant to California law, every registered voter must be mailed an official ballot. We will start delivering ballots to the U.S. Postal Service by Oct. 5. • Vote Early by Mail or Drop-Off: Mark your ballot at home, place it in the return envelope, seal, and sign. Return it using one of three secure methods by the deadline: Put it in an official ballot drop box, mail it (postmarked by Nov. 3), or drop it off at a Vote Center before 8 p.m. on Nov. 3. Learn more at ocvote.gov/options .• Vote In Person (Oct. 24-Nov. 3): Visit any full-service Vote Center in the county during the in-person voting period. Select centers open daily beginning Oct. 24. All centers open from Oct. 31 through Nov. 3. Vote early to avoid possible lines on the last day. For locations and hours, visit ocvote.gov/votecenter .We work hard to make voting convenient and safe for all Orange County voters. Did you know you can mark your ballot at home, bring it to any Vote Center, sign in, and scan it? You can also mark a newly printed ballot on site. At our Vote Centers, we also help voters update their registration and assist voters with disabilities and those whose preferred language is not English to vote independently. Learn more at ocvote.gov and in this Voter Information Guide. If you can’t find an answer to a question in this Guide or on our website, please call the Registrar of Voters’ Voter Hotline at 714-567-7600 or 1-888-628-6837, or email us at ocvoter@ocvote.gov. Note: A separate State Voter Information Guide will be mailed to you, which contains information about State Propositions and candidates for State Constitutional offices. Sincerely, Bob Page Registrar of Voters 

> Need Help? (714) 567-7600 or ocvote.gov/help

@ocregistrar facebook.com/ocrov ocvote.gov ocregistrar Election Integrity 

Election security is a top priority for the Orange County Registrar of Voters. More than 125 security measures are used for every election, including: 

Physical 

Numbered security seals cover every opening on each piece of voting system equipment to prevent and detect tampering. 

Technical 

All voting system equipment is kept entirely offline and is not connected to the internet or any other external network, eliminating remote access risks. 

Process 

Ballots from at least one percent of randomly selected precincts are hand counted to confirm the accuracy of election results across every contest on the ballot. 

Voter List 

Voter registrations are updated within five business days whenever OCROV is notified of a change by the voter or an official government source. Learn more at ocvote.gov/security Vote Easy. Vote Secure. 

# USE YOUR VOICE 

# FOR VOTER’S CHOICE 

The Registrar of Voters wants Orange County voters to tell us what you think about how we conduct elections and how we might improve access to voting. In 2025, we are reviewing and updating our Election Administration Plan. The California Voter’s Choice Act requires our Plan to include our strategy for: Voter education and outreach • Vote Centers and Ballot Drop Boxes Language and accessibility services • Security and contingency plans To read the current 2021-2025 Plan, submit suggestions, and sign up on our Interested Parties email list to receive updates including community meeting announcements, please visit ocvote.gov/eap .

# SIGN UP FOR NEWSLETTERS 

The Orange County Registrar of Voters provides valuable information about the current election, the steps we take to ensure your vote is counted accurately and kept secure, and upcoming events through our newsletters. To have it delivered directly to your inbox or phone, you can sign up at ocvote.gov/signup .

